## Summary of the Patch Modifications

### File: `drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/mac.c`

**Modification:**  
The call to `wiphy_work_flush()` for each ath12k radio (ar) is moved from inside the `if (!vif)` block to a position before that block, so it is executed unconditionally for every `ath12k_mac_op_flush()` call.

**Old code (simplified):**
```c
if (!vif) {
    for_each_ar(ah, ar, i)
        ath12k_mac_flush(ar, vif, drops);
    return;
}
for_each_ar(ah, ar, i)
    wiphy_work_flush(hw->wiphy, &ar->wmi_mgmt_tx_work);
```

**New code (simplified):**
```c
for_each_ar(ah, ar, i)
    wiphy_work_flush(hw->wiphy, &ar->wmi_mgmt_tx_work);

if (!vif) {
    for_each_ar(ah, ar, i)
        ath12k_mac_flush(ar, vif, drops);
    return;
}
```

### Relationships Between Modifications

- The change ensures that pending management frame transmission work items (`wmi_mgmt_tx_work`) are flushed **before** the driver waits for the flush to complete, regardless of whether the flush is for a specific virtual interface (non-NULL vif) or for the whole hardware (NULL vif).  
- Previously, when `vif == NULL` the code returned early without flushing the work, causing a deadlock: the flush process held the wiphy lock waiting for management frame transmission, but the transmission work could not run because it also required the wiphy lock.  
- By moving the `wiphy_work_flush()` call to the top of the function, the lock inversion is avoided and the flush proceeds correctly for all cases.

